course: dictionary
lesson: 7198
title: Petition: asking for change
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: A petition is a written request that many people sign together to ask someone in charge to do or change something. «A petition is a letter many people sign to ask for a change.» The word signed is the clue: one name is a wish, but a page full of names is a stronger message.
explain: Petition can also be a verb. To petition someone is to make a formal request to them, often with a petition. «The more people sign it, the harder it is for those in charge to ignore.» A petition is a peaceful tool: it asks, it does not fight.
explain: The word has other meanings too. In law, a petition is a formal written request to a court. In church, a prayer of petition asks God for something. Read the words around it: is the request going to the council, a judge, or God?
fact: A petition is a written request, signed by many people, asking someone in power to do or change something.
fact: To petition someone means to make a formal request to them, often with a petition.
fact: «Sign a petition», «start a petition» and «petition the council» are useful phrases.
fact: In law, «a petition for divorce» is a formal written request to a court.
fact: Suffragists campaigned for votes for women with petitions, meetings and peaceful marches, and Millicent Fawcett led them in Britain.
exercise: «A petition is a letter many people sign to ask for a change.» What is a petition? | A loud shout across the playground | *A written request signed by many people, asking for a change | A kind of sweet | A court that makes the laws
exercise: Your class wants a new crossing near school. Which peaceful way can you use to ask for it? | Throw stones at the council offices | Hide the traffic cones | *Start a petition and collect signatures | Ignore the council and wait
exercise: «The prayers of petition ask God for help.» What does petition mean here? | A list of school rules | A loud song | *A prayer that asks God for something | A dance performed in church
test: «Suffragists campaigned for votes for women with petitions, meetings and peaceful marches.» Which of these is a peaceful way to campaign in this sentence? | Breaking windows | *Petitions | Hiding from the crowds | Shouting at the police
test: In «to petition the council», what does petition mean? | To buy a new council building | To vote for the council | To fight the council | *To make a formal request to the council
exam: «Ordinary people signed petitions until the law changed.» In this sentence, what are petitions? | Sweets that are made without sugar | Lessons at school | Long walks in the country | *Written lists of names asking for a change
